## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or `Join(timeout)` |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. The runtime no longer injects `ThreadAbortException`, so your catch block never fires and your `Stop()` method crashes.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — replaces ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; we simply continue to loop exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give thread time to exit gracefully
        if (_workerThread != null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
            {
                // Thread didn't exit — it's stuck in uncooperative code.
                // Log a warning; you cannot force-kill it in modern .NET.
                Console.Error.WriteLine("Worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
            }
        }

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — signals cancellation cooperatively.

2. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (...)`** — the `when` guard ensures you only catch your own cancellation, not unrelated ones.

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** — unnecessary; after catching `OperationCanceledException`, the loop condition `!token.IsCancellationRequested` naturally exits.

4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(timeout)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of sleeping blindly.

5. **Token passed to `GetNextItem`** — critical! If that method blocks (e.g., on a `BlockingCollection`), it must accept a token to unblock.

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| `GetNextItem()` blocks without a token | `Stop()` hangs forever — cancellation is cooperative, blocking calls won't wake up on their own |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` guard | Accidentally swallows cancellation from unrelated subsystems |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | Work between checkpoints still completes; add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |

### If `GetNextItem` Uses a BlockingCollection

```csharp
// Before (blocks indefinitely):
private object GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After (cancellation-aware):
private object GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
```

If `GetNextItem` does synchronous I/O that can't accept a token, you'll need to refactor it to an async alternative or use a signaling mechanism (e.g., add a poison-pill item to the queue).
